+# Decision: Delivery transport for committed board changes
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard holds one authoritative revision per committed edit; editors submit commands over HTTPS and browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers watch a given event, with 10-25 editors per market and bursts of up to 20 edits per second around opening time. Something has to carry each committed revision from the server to those browsers.
+
+Any transport chosen here constrains three downstream design nodes together: the change store's shape (whether it needs a replay-friendly revision log), delivery's internal state (whether it holds live sessions or just answers one-off requests), and browser sync's recovery logic (whether a reconnect needs to do more than re-fetch latest state). The transport is a single, central choice the rest of the board's realtime behavior is sized around.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Freshness goal (background, not a gate).** The HLD's 15-second committed-change visibility goal and 20-edits/second burst target are met by every option in contention — they narrow the field only in how much margin each leaves, not by eliminating any option.
+- **Vendor cost (major).** Whether delivery carries a recurring per-connection or per-message bill to an external provider, against the HLD tenet that self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted — admissible either way, but a real cost to weigh.
+- **Operational ownership (major).** Whether the project takes on connection-capacity planning and reconnect handling itself, versus pushing that work onto a vendor's infrastructure or avoiding persistent connections entirely.
+- **Provider coupling (moderate).** Whether the board's core delivery path depends on a specific vendor's API and operational continuity.
+- **Latency margin (minor).** How much headroom an option leaves against the 15-second freshness goal, since all options already clear it.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers issue a cursor-based HTTPS request on a five-second timer; the server answers with any revisions committed since the browser's last-seen cursor. No persistent connection is held.
+
+- Vendor cost: **strong fit**. No persistent-connection infrastructure and no vendor bill — requests are answered by the same stateless HTTPS layer access already uses.
+- Operational ownership: **strong fit**. Nothing to keep alive; a dropped request simply retries on the next tick. Connection-capacity planning for long-lived sessions doesn't arise.
+- Provider coupling: **strong fit**. No third party in the delivery path.
+- Latency margin: **weak fit**. Up to five seconds of polling interval plus request latency eats directly into the 15-second goal, leaving the thinnest margin of the three options and no room to absorb bursts gracefully.
+
+Summary: cheapest and simplest to operate, at the cost of the least freshness headroom and the most repeated reads under load.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server holds an open WebSocket per connected browser session and pushes each committed revision to affected sessions as it commits. The project operates the socket infrastructure itself.
+
+- Vendor cost: **strong fit**. No recurring per-connection or per-message fee to an external realtime provider.
+- Operational ownership: **weak fit**. The project owns connection-capacity planning for up to 800 concurrent sockets per event, plus reconnect and catch-up handling when a session drops.
+- Provider coupling: **strong fit**. No vendor sits in the delivery path; the board's realtime behavior is not contingent on a third party's API or uptime.
+- Latency margin: **strong fit**. Revisions push immediately on commit, leaving the most headroom against the 15-second goal and the cleanest handling of opening-time bursts.
+
+Summary: the most latency headroom and the least vendor exposure, paid for with connection and reconnect operations the project must build and run itself.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A managed realtime/pub-sub vendor relays committed revisions to browser subscriptions; the server publishes to the vendor's API instead of holding sessions itself.
+
+- Vendor cost: **weak fit**. Recurring per-connection or per-message fees scale with the 800-browser, multi-event footprint.
+- Operational ownership: **strong fit**. The vendor owns connection capacity and reconnect handling; the project only publishes and reconciles.
+- Provider coupling: **weak fit**. The core delivery path depends on the vendor's subscription model, API, and continued availability.
+- Latency margin: **strong fit**. Comparable to self-hosted sockets — revisions push as they commit.
+
+Summary: the least operational burden and strong latency, in exchange for a recurring bill and a vendor dependency sitting on the board's critical delivery path.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets is the choice. Against the major criteria, it ties hosted realtime on latency margin and ties polling on vendor cost, but is the only option that is a strong fit on both **vendor cost** and **provider coupling** at once, with the strongest latency margin of the three. Polling was eliminated on **latency margin**: at up to 25 editors per market and 20-edit/second opening bursts, a five-second interval leaves too little room before the 15-second goal, and the repeated-read pattern compounds with 800 simultaneous browsers. Hosted realtime was eliminated on **vendor cost** and **provider coupling**: a recurring bill and a vendor in the critical delivery path were judged to outweigh the operational relief it buys, given the HLD tenet that self-hosted infrastructure is explicitly permitted and not disfavored.
+
+The decision turns on that tenet directly — self-hosted infrastructure being an admissible answer, not a fallback, is what makes trading operational ownership for vendor independence a live option rather than a forced one.
+
+Committing to this forecloses a simple stateless delivery layer: delivery must now hold live session state and own reconnect handling, rather than just answering one-off requests. It obligates the change store to keep revisions replayable (so a reconnecting session can catch up rather than only ever seeing "latest"), and it obligates browser sync to carry reconnect and catch-up logic rather than a bare subscribe-and-render. Those three nodes are sized around this choice, not around either alternative.
